          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is generalized anxiety disorder, and how is it different from ordinary worry?",
          "answer": "Generalized anxiety disorder (GAD) is long-lasting, excessive worry about many everyday matters. The reference summary describes it as worry that occurs more days than not for at least six months, that the person finds hard to control, and that comes with physical and mental symptoms. The worry moves from topic to topic instead of staying attached to one situation. (Source: *Generalized anxiety disorder*, general clinical reference.)\n\nAnxiety itself is normal and useful. The reference explains that it becomes a disorder when it is:\n\n- **Excessive:** out of proportion to the circumstances.\n- **Persistent:** it continues when the circumstances change.\n- **Impairing:** it is costly to daily functioning.\n\nThe usual accompanying symptoms include restlessness, tiring easily, trouble concentrating, irritability, muscle tension, and disturbed sleep. The same source notes that these features are what separate it from ordinary worry, which most people experience at some point and which usually fades when the situation passes.\n\nIn the ICD-10-CM classification, anxiety disorders sit in the F40 to F48 range of the mental, behavioral and neurodevelopmental chapter, alongside phobias, panic disorder and obsessive-compulsive disorder. The retrieved passages do not give the specific code for GAD, so I cannot name it here. A clinician or medical coder can confirm it.\n\nOnly a clinician can tell whether these features apply to a particular person. If worry is persistent, hard to control, or getting in the way of work, sleep or relationships, it is reasonable to raise it with a doctor or other health professional. Screening questionnaires, such as the seven-item GAD questionnaire described in the guidance on anxiety screening, are one way clinicians assess severity. Those results are only a starting point for a professional assessment.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
